That's why I added the advice to start in Logo if
going for "self taught". That's a language designed
to be comprehended by an undeveloped mind, and it is
supremely easy to start slowly and build skills
because the turtle graphics makes seeing "what's
wrong" easy in a way separate from textual analysis,
that appeals to separate skill kits.

Everyone IMO should _attempt_ to learn logo. If
learned, can Scheme or OCaml be far behind? Logo
teaches _much_ better K.I.S.S. programming habits,
too, than does starting in C.

If Logo doesn't work out, there's no real reason to
keep on trying; you're probably "coding blind".

